# Routes and Handlers

## Overview

Routes decide what an Agent does with each Signal. Jido reads routes in
declaration order and runs the first matching handler. Each route has an
explicit handler or an explicit forward destination.

```text
order.create      -> Routes.Record          Action
order.checkout    -> Routes.Checkout        Flow
partner.order     -> inline transform       Signal rewrite
work.request      -> agent(:worker)         forward
work.completed    -> parent()               forward
```

There is no bare string route. A string path must name an Action, a Flow, an
inline handler, or use `forward`.

## Install

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido, "~> 3.0.0-beta.2"}])
```

## Actions and Flows

```elixir
defmodule Routes.Record do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "record",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{label: Zoi.string()})

  @impl true
  def run(%{label: label}, %{agent_state: state}) do
    {:ok, %{state | last: label}}
  end
end

defmodule Routes.Total do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "total",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{price: Zoi.integer(), quantity: Zoi.integer()})

  @impl true
  def run(%{price: price, quantity: quantity}, %{agent_state: state}) do
    {:ok, %{state | total: price * quantity}}
  end
end

defmodule Routes.Checkout do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "checkout",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{price: Zoi.integer(), quantity: Zoi.integer()})

  flow do
    step "total",
      action: Routes.Total,
      params: %{price: input(:price), quantity: input(:quantity)}

    output result("total")
  end
end
```

An Action or Flow route must return the complete next Agent state.

## The Route Table

```elixir
defmodule Routes.Orders do
  use Jido.Agent, name: "orders"

  state do
    schema(
      Zoi.object(%{
        last: Zoi.string() |> Zoi.default(""),
        total: Zoi.integer() |> Zoi.default(0),
        notes: Zoi.list(Zoi.string()) |> Zoi.default([])
      })
    )
  end

  routes do
    signal_source("/guides/routes")

    route "order.create", Routes.Record,
      as: :create,
      defaults: %{label: "created"}

    route "order.checkout", Routes.Checkout, as: :checkout

    route "order.note", as: :note do
      action %{text: text},
        schema: Zoi.object(%{text: Zoi.string()}),
        context: context do
        {:ok, %{context.agent_state | notes: [text | context.agent_state.notes]}}
      end
    end

    route "partner.order", as: :partner do
      transform %Jido.Signal{data: %{ref: ref}} = signal when is_binary(ref) do
        {:ok, %{signal | type: "order.create", data: %{label: "partner:" <> ref}}}
      end
    end

    route "order.*", Routes.Record, defaults: %{label: "wildcard"}
  end
end
```

| Route part | Rule |
| --- | --- |
| path | An exact Signal type, or a pattern with `*` or `**`. It must be unique in the Agent. |
| handler | An Action, a Flow, one inline `action`, or one inline `transform`. |
| `as:` | Generates `<name>_signal/2` and `<name>_signal!/2`. Exact paths only. |
| `defaults:` | Adds missing Action or Flow input values. Signal data wins. |

## SignalAction Routes

A SignalAction can own the path and the handler:

```elixir
route MyApp.Ask
```

The input Signal type from `MyApp.Ask` becomes the route path. To use its
Signal type with a different handler, use the two-module form:

```elixir
route MyApp.Ask, MyApp.Turn
```

This form runs `MyApp.Turn`. It does not run `MyApp.Ask` first.

## Explicit Forwarding

Use `forward` when a route only sends the input Signal to another Agent:

```elixir
routes do
  forward "work.request", to: agent(:worker)
  forward "work.completed", to: parent()
end
```

`agent(:worker)` must name a member in the same Agent definition. `parent()`
means the current runtime parent. Forward targets are portable data:

```elixir
%{kind: :forward, target: %{kind: :agent, name: :worker}}
%{kind: :forward, target: %{kind: :parent}}
```

A forward does not use a channel, a topology connection, `default_dispatch:`,
or self-dispatch. If the named Agent or parent is not available, the Turn
returns a clear error.

## Transforms

A transform returns one changed Signal and does not change Agent state. Jido
normalizes the result to an `Emit` Directive.

```elixir
signal = Routes.Orders.partner_signal!(%{ref: "A-17"})
{:ok, candidate, [emit]} = Routes.Orders.cmd(
  Routes.Orders.instantiate!(id: "orders-1"),
  signal
)

{candidate.state.last, emit.signal.type}
```

The result is `{"", "order.create"}`. The new Signal starts a separate Turn
when a live Agent Server dispatches it.

## Wildcards and Order

`*` matches one segment. `**` matches any number of segments. The first match
wins, so put exact paths before wildcard paths.

## Defaults and Helpers

`defaults:` performs a shallow merge under Signal data. `as: :create`
generates `create_signal/2` and `create_signal!/2`. These functions build a
Signal and do not run a Turn.

## Compile-Time Errors

Jido rejects:

- a string route without a handler;
- two routes with the same path;
- `as:` on a wildcard path;
- the same helper name on two routes;
- `defaults:` on a transform or forward;
- a target module together with an inline block;
- more than one inline handler; and
- old route options such as `via:`.

